Z||000000||geyser||04-16-2000||12:23 AM||||Installed a JC Whitney rear swaybar on my 87 GT last week. Installation was simple and quick. It has been the best suspension improvement so far. The car corners flat and the bump steer is almost non existant. Highly recommended. ||||reg||10
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||04-16-2000||03:13 AM||||how much $?||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||04-16-2000||08:49 AM||||The Addco sway bars are about $95 plus shipping, although you generally can subtract some sort of 10% or 15% discount if you look at their catalog cover. I tried to order one a month ago, but the Addco company had recently moved and was extremely backlogged on orders. Geyser, if you just bought and received this, then that's good news. Fiero owners rejoice, because this is the best price on a Fiero rear sway bar you can find.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||04-16-2000||09:32 AM||||I bought the Koni rear sway bar from the Fiero Store (part of the entire KYB kit) They normally sell for $149.00. Hey Batboy what is the size of the Addco? The Koni is 1" for rear and 1 1/4" for front. Just curious if the bars are same size.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||04-16-2000||10:47 AM||||The JC Whitney Addco rear sway bar is 7/8" (I think), I actually ended up buying mine from the Fiero Store, but it's not the Koni bar. Mine is 7/8" too, but I don't know what brand it is. I believe a 7/8" rear bar is about as big as you want use, if the front sway bar is stock. If you go with a bigger front sway bar like Oreif did, then you can increase the diameter of the rear bar too. How much stiffer is it, Oreif?<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 04-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||04-17-2000||12:20 AM||||I'd checked with JCwhitney about a bar about a couple weeks ago,and they said the kit was on backorder until 4-20,so now would be a good time to check back probably.<BR>Roughly,the front bar should be 1/8" larger than the rear.Since my springs are stiff enough,and I don't want to bind up the suspension any more than absolutely needed,I will try the 7/8" bar with the stock front bar,and maybe even wind up grinding a little off the bars until I get it all working right.This would be only after new sticky tires,and some good evaluation. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ozzy||04-17-2000||12:27 AM||||Glad to hear this was a 'solid' modification to an '87 GT. Mine kinda handles like a garbage bag o' yogurt! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  It sticks real good but just feels sloppy. I need to rebuild the entire suspension to do it right. Interested in lowCG's balljoint mod actually, so I'll be a callin' when I'm ready. Gotta save up some pescos first.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000007||geyser||04-23-2000||01:53 AM||||Sorry I did not post any answers to questions on the swaybar, I've been out of town. The price was $95.00 plus shipping. The size is 7/8", which is appropriate match for the front 1" sway bar. I'm curious, Orief, how the larger swaybars performed? I used urethane bushings at the mounts and at the endlinks. The positioning of the bar was slightly different than the instructions and I had to use a shorter length bolt on one side each, on the mounts. I'm still very happy with the outcome.||||reg||1
